<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Fidenae</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 426 BCE (approx.) · capital Fidenae</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small town on the Tiber just north of Rome, allied with Veii, which Rome fought for repeatedly and finally destroyed in 426 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Fidenae stood on a hill above the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>, about 8 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, opposite the Etruscan city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/veii">Veii</a>. It was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> town, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> says it had Etruscan connections, and settlement there goes back to the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. Its position on the river and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-salaria">Via Salaria</a> made it strategically vital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of its government. Roman tradition tells of repeated wars from the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a>, as Fidenae shifted between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/veii">Veii</a>. In 438 BCE it went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lars-tolumnius">Lars Tolumnius</a> of Veii and killed Roman envoys; the Roman officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulus-cornelius-cossus">Aulus Cornelius Cossus</a> killed Tolumnius in single combat and dedicated his armour as the spolia opima.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 426 BCE the dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamercus-aemilius-mamercinus">Mamercus Aemilius</a> captured and destroyed Fidenae, and its land became Roman. It survived only as a village; under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, in 27 CE, the collapse of a wooden amphitheatre there reportedly killed thousands, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> records.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/fidenae.jpg" alt="Fidenae" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur (talk) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Latium_-5th_Century_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 426 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fidenae</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Latin polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-563&amp;focus=fidenae" aria-label="Fidenae on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4984 683 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Fidenae lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5304 939L5301 938M5304 939L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5301 938h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 939h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 939h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-563&amp;focus=fidenae">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

